WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.840
A cyber security model with a 1 million token context window just launched with perfect scores on professional CTF tasks. This is Models &amp; Agents, Episode 200, Episode 200. Thanks for sticking with us. Orca router released Orca Cyber Zero 1.5 as the successor to Orca Cyber Zero 1.0. The model targets vulnerability reproduction, exploit development, and penetration testing. It includes a 1 million token context window, native function calling, and structured outputs.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:37.800
inputs. It reports 100% on side bench across 39 of 40 tasks and 95.8% on the available subset

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:44.960
of CVE bench. Pricing stands at $3 per million input tokens and $7.50 per million output

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:50.580
tokens through the hosted API only. It supports 128K max output.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:56.680
The three design goals center on finding unknown flaws such as remote code execution, sandbox

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:01.740
box escapes, authentication bypasses, privilege escalation, and attack chains.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:07.180
The second goal focuses on reasoning through attack paths and ranking flaws by demonstrable

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:08.080
exploitability.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.580
The third goal builds the model for autonomous agents with the large context window, native

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.720
tool calling, and extended reasoning for large code bases.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:21.940
The model is the successor to the version that shipped on September 17, 2026.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:29.140
It scores 93.9% on HumanEval Plus and 76.5% on SweetBench Pro V2.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:36.080
Users can now add version 3.15 to GitHub Actions testing matrices to run tests against the

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:36.980
new Python release.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.240
The update landed after repeated checks of the actions slash Python versions repository.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:47.440
The author had been waiting for this for a couple of days and even told chat GPT to check

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:47.860
for it.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:54.260
Developers can now add 3.15 to a Github actions testing matrix to run tests against the

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:56.820
new Python 3.15.0 release.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:03.020
A one-line formula shows how often an LLM's top token flips even at temperature zero.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:09.000
Identical completions begin to diverge after roughly 100 tokens even at temperature zero.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:13.180
The formula explains the rate at which an LLM's top token flips.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:17.260
This non-determinism appears even when temperature is set to zero.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:21.400
The post provides a concrete way to calculate the frequency of these flips.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:28.860
The article recommends adding architectural guardrails around how agents ingest and use untrusted sources inside workflows.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:33.720
It focuses on preventing prompt injection and data poisoning during tool use.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:38.440
The guidance centers on safe ingestion of external data by autonomous agents.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:45.240
Safety prompts improve the clinical decision quality of AI models by steering outputs away from unsafe recommendations.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:49.840
The approach requires only prompt-level changes rather than retraining.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:54.960
The method helps AI models make safer clinical choices through the addition of safety prompts.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.640
These prompts steer model outputs away from unsafe recommendations in medical decision contexts.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.880
The technique works at the prompt level without requiring model retraining.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.320
Pop the hood on structured output constraints in agent loops and the mechanism works by constraining

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.540
the token sampler at each step so only schema valid tokens remain possible.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:20.660
This adds roughly 50 to 100 milliseconds of latency per turn, but cuts downstream parsing

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:23.680
failures by more than half in long horizon agent runs.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:29.720
The trade-off appears most clearly above 8,000 tokens of accumulated context, where free

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:32.260
text drift compounds faster than schema drift.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:37.140
The constraint is applied at the sampling stage, so invalid tokens are never generated.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.920
In practice, this means the model cannot produce free-form texts that would break downstream

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:42.580
parsers.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:49.080
The latency cost is modest at 50-100 milliseconds per turn, yet the reduction in parsing failures

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.700
exceeds 50% in extended agent sessions.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:57.140
The benefit grows with context length because free-text outputs accumulate inconsistencies

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:00.280
more rapidly once context exceeds 8,000 tokens.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:04.800
Teams should reach for structured outputs when the agent must hand results to another

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:05.800
tool or database.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:11.540
FreeTex remains preferable for exploratory reasoning steps that do not feed further automation.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:18.420
Try Orca Cyber 01.5 through the Orca Router API for authorized vulnerability research tasks,

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:22.460
where a 1 million token context helps analyze large code bases.

